[258.347175, "o", "--- Lopinavir-Ritonavir PASSAGE_0001 ---\r\n'2.1 General Administration Recommendations\\nLopinavir\\xa0and ritonavir oral solution must be taken with food.'\r\n--- Lopinavir-Ritonavir PASSAGE_0005 ---\r\n'Recommended Dosage\\n80 mg/20 mg per mL Oral Solution 400 mg/100 mg (5 mL)\\xa0twice daily\\nThe dose of lopinavir and ritonavir must be increased when administered in combination with efavirenz, nevirapine or nelfinavir. Table 3 outlines the dosage recommendations for twice daily dosing when lopinavir and ritonavir is taken in combination with these agents.'\r\n--- Lopinavir-Ritonavir PASSAGE_0014 ---\r\n'5.1 Risk of Serious Adverse Reactions Due to Drug Interactions\\nInitiation of lopinavir and ritonavir, a CYP3A inhibitor, in patients receiving medications metabolized by CYP3A or initiation of medications metabolized by CYP3A in patients already receiving lopinavir\\xa0and ritonavir, may increase plasma concentrations of medications metabolized by CYP3A. \\xa0Initiation of medications that inhibit or induce CYP3A may increase or decrease concentrations of lopinavir\\xa0and ritonavir, respectively. These interactions may lead to:\\n- Clinically significant adverse reactions, potentially leading to severe, life-threatening, or fatal events from greater exposures of concomitant medications.\\n- Clinically significant adverse reactions from greater exposures of lopinavir\\xa0and ritonavir.\\n- Loss of therapeutic effect of lopinavir\\xa0and ritonavir and possible development of resistance.\\nSee Table 12 for steps to prevent or manage these possible and known significant drug interactions, including dosing recommendations . Consider the potential for drug interactions prior to and during lopinavir\\xa0and ritonavir therapy; review concomitant medications during lopinavir\\xa0and ritonavir therapy, and monitor for the adverse reactions associated with the concomitant medications .'\r\n--- Lopinavir-Ritonavir PASSAGE_0016 ---\r\n'5.3 Pancreatitis\\nPancreatitis has been observed in patients receiving lopinavir\\xa0and ritonavir therapy, including those who developed marked triglyceride elevations. In some cases, fatalities have been observed. Although a causal relationship to lopinavir\\xa0and ritonavir has not been established, marked triglyceride elevations are a risk factor for development of pancreatitis . Patients with advanced HIV-1 disease may be at increased risk of elevated triglycerides and pancreatitis, and patients with a history of pancreatitis may be at increased risk for recurrence during lopinavir\\xa0and ritonavir therapy.\\nPancreatitis should be considered if clinical symptoms (nausea, vomiting, abdominal pain) or abnormalities in laboratory values (such as increased serum lipase or amylase values) suggestive of pancreatitis occur. Patients who exhibit these signs or symptoms should be evaluated and lopinavir\\xa0and ritonavir and/or other antiretroviral therapy should be suspended as clinically appropriate.'\r\n--- Lopinavir-Ritonavir PASSAGE_0028 ---\r\n'Adverse Reactions in Adults\\nThe safety of lopinavir\\xa0and ritonavir has been investigated in about 2,600 patients in Phase II-IV clinical trials, of which about 700 have received a dose of 800/200 mg (6 capsules or 4 tablets) once daily. Along with nucleoside reverse transcriptase inhibitors (NRTIs), in some studies, lopinavir\\xa0and ritonavir was used in combination with efavirenz or nevirapine.\\nIn cl'\r\n--- Lopinavir-Ritonavir PASSAGE_0018 ---\r\n'5.5 QT Interval Prolongation\\nPostmarketing cases of QT interval prolongation and torsade de pointes have been reported although causality of lopinavir\\xa0and ritonavir could not be established. Avoid use in patients with congenital long QT syndrome, those with hypokalemia, and with other drugs that prolong the QT interval .'\r\n--- Lopinavir-Ritonavir PASSAGE_0019 ---\r\n"]
[258.347237, "o", "'5.6 PR Interval Prolongation\\nLopinavir\\xa0and ritonavir prolongs the PR interval in some patients. Cases of second or third degree atrioventricular block have been reported. Lopinavir\\xa0and ritonavir should be used with caution in patients with underlying structural heart disease, pre-existing conduction system abnormalities, ischemic heart disease or cardiomyopathies, as these patients may be at increased risk for developing cardiac conduction abnormalities.\\nThe impact on the PR interval of co-administration of lopinavir\\xa0and ritonavir with other drugs that prolong the PR interval (including calcium channel blockers, beta-adrenergic blockers, digoxin and atazanavir) has not been evaluated. As a result, co-administration of lopinavir\\xa0and ritonavir with these drugs should be undertaken with caution, particularly with those drugs metabolized by CYP3A. Clinical monitoring is recommended .'\r\n--- Paxlovid PASSAGE_0013 ---\r\n'5.1\\tRisk of Serious Adverse Reactions Due to Drug Interactions\\nInitiation of PAXLOVID, which contains ritonavir, a strong CYP3A inhibitor, in patients receiving medications metabolized by CYP3A or initiation of medications metabolized by CYP3A in patients already receiving PAXLOVID, may increase plasma concentrations of medications metabolized by CYP3A. Medications that induce CYP3A may decrease concentrations of PAXLOVID. These interactions may lead to:\\n- Clinically significant adverse reactions, potentially leading to severe, life-threatening, or fatal events from greater exposures of concomitant medications.\\n- Loss of therapeutic effect of PAXLOVID and possible development of viral resistance.\\nSevere, life-threatening, and/or fatal adverse reactions due to drug interactions have been reported in patients treated with PAXLOVID. The most commonly reported concomitant medications resulting in serious adverse reactions were calcineurin inhibitors (e.g., tacrolimus, cyclosporine), followed by calcium channel blockers.\\nPrior to prescribing PAXLOVID, review all medications taken by the patient to assess potential drug-drug interactions and determine if concomitant medications require a dose adjustment, interruption, and/or additional monitoring (e.g., calcineurin inhibitors) . See Table 2 for clinically significant drug interactions, including contraindicated drugs. Drugs listed in Table 2 are a guide and not considered a comprehensive list of all possible drugs that may interact with PAXLOVID.\\nConsider the benefit of PAXLOVID treatment in reducing hospitalization and death, and whether the risk of potential drug-drug interactions for an individual patient can be appropriately managed .'\r\n--- Paxlovid PASSAGE_0014 ---\r\n'5.2\\tHypersensitivity Reactions\\nAnaphylaxis, serious skin reactions (including toxic epidermal necrolysis and Stevens-Johnson syndrome), and other hypersensitivity reactions have been reported with PAXLOVID . If signs and symptoms of a clinically significant hypersensitivity reaction or anaphylaxis occur, immediately discontinue PAXLOVID and initiate appropriate medications and/or supportive care.'\r\n--- Paxlovid PASSAGE_0016 ---\r\n'5.4\\tRisk of HIV-1 Resistance Development\\nBecause nirmatrelvir is co-administered with ritonavir, there may be a risk of HIV-1 developing resistance to HIV protease inhibitors in individuals with uncontrolled or undiagnosed HIV-1 infection .'\r\n"]
[258.349696, "o", "\u001b[?2004hroot@a1980178bfd8:/workspace# "]
[277.370661, "i", "cat << 'EOF' > verify_quotes_2.py\nimport json\n\nwith open('/workspace/data/public/labels.jsonl') as f:\n    labels = {l['set_id']: l for l in [json.loads(line) for line in f]}\n\npaxlovid_id = '8a99d6d6-fd9e-45bb-b1bf-48c7f761232a'\nlop_rit_id = '4b1caefa-e6a2-42f3-9194-6dc1dd9e6d85'\n\ntest_quotes = [\n    (paxlovid_id, 0, \"PAXLOVID includes ritonavir, a strong CYP3A inhibitor, which may lead to greater exposure of certain concomitant medications, resulting in potentially severe, life-threatening, or fatal events\"),\n    (paxlovid_id, 19, \"PAXLOVID (nirmatrelvir co-packaged with ritonavir) is a strong inhibitor of CYP3A, and an inhibitor of CYP2D6, P-gp and OATP1B1.\"),\n    (paxlovid_id, 20, \"Nirmatrelvir and ritonavir are CYP3A substrates; therefore, drugs that induce CYP3A may decrease nirmatrelvir and ritonavir plasma concentrations and reduce PAXLOVID therapeutic effect\"),\n    (paxlovid_id, 12, \"PAXLOVID is contraindicated with drugs that are primarily metabolized by CYP3A and for which elevated concentrations are associated with serious and/or life-threatening reactions and drugs that are strong CYP3A inducers\"),\n    (lop_rit_id, 1, \"oral solution must be taken with food.\"),\n    (lop_rit_id, 2, \"Because lopinavir and ritonavir oral solution contains ethanol and propylene glycol, it is not recommended for use with polyurethane feeding tubes due to potential incompatibility.\"),\n    (lop_rit_id, 7, \"The dose of the oral solution should be administered using the calibrated cup (supplied) or oral dosing syringe.\"),\n    (lop_rit_id, 3, \"Lopinavir and ritonavir can be given in once daily or twice daily dosing regimen\"),\n    (lop_rit_id, 4, \"80 mg/20 mg per mL Oral Solution 800 mg/200 mg (10 mL) once daily\"),\n    (lop_rit_id, 5, \"The dose of lopinavir and ritonavir must be increased when administered in combination with efavirenz, nevirapine or nelfinavir.\"),\n    (lop_rit_id, 6, \"80 mg/20 mg per mL Oral Solution 520 mg/130 mg (6.5 mL) twice daily\"),\n    (lop_rit_id, 14, \"Initiation of lopinavir and ritonavir, a CYP3A inhibitor, in patients receiving medications metabolized by CYP3A\"),\n    (lop_rit_id, 15, \"Lopinavir and ritonavir oral solution contains the excipients ethanol, approximately 42% (v/v) and propylene glycol, approximately 15% (w/v).\"),\n    (lop_rit_id, 16, \"Pancreatitis has been observed in patients receiving\"),\n    (lop_rit_id, 28, \"reported ongoing diarrhea.\"),\n    (lop_rit_id, 18, \"Avoid use in patients with congenital long QT syndrome\"),\n    (lop_rit_id, 19, \"Cases of second or third degree atrioventricular block have been reported.\"),\n    (paxlovid_id, 13, \"Medications that induce CYP3A may decrease concentrations of PAXLOVID.\"),\n    (paxlovid_id, 14, \"immediately discontinue PAXLOVID and initiate appropriate medications and/or supportive care.\"),\n    (paxlovid_id, 15, \"Hepatic transaminase elevations, clinical hepatitis, and jaundice have occurred in patients receiving ritonavir.\"),\n    (paxlovid_id, 16, \"Because nirmatrelvir is co-administered with ritonavir\"),\n    (paxlovid_id, 18, \"The most common adverse reactions (≥1% incidence in the PAXLOVID group and occurring at a greater frequency than in the placebo group) were dysgeusia (5% and <1%, respectively) and diarrhea (3% and 2%, respectively).\")\n]\n\nfor set_id, idx, quote in test_quotes:\n    chunk = labels[set_id]['chunks'][idx]\n    if quote in chunk:\n        print(f\"OK: {set_id[:8]} PASSAGE_{idx:04d}\")\n    else:\n        print(f\"FAIL: {set_id[:8]} PASSAGE_{idx:04d}\")\n        print(f\"  Expected quote: {repr(quote)}\")\n        print(f\"  Chunk start: {repr(chunk[:300])}\")\nEOF\npython3 verify_quotes_2.py\n"]
